<<<
PT-. In ptarmigan, & in Ptolemy & its derivatives, the p is always silent. In other words the OED favours its being sounded; cf. Ps-. But ptomaine is perhaps the only one sufficiently common in talk for the pronunciation to matter.
===
**PT-.** In *ptarmigan*, & in *Ptolemy* & its derivatives, the p is always silent. In other words the OED favours its being sounded; cf. ^^PS-^^. But *ptomaine* is perhaps the only one sufficiently common in talk for the pronunciation to matter.
>>>

<<<
ptomaine. The OED stigmatizes toma'n as an illiterate pronunciation; but, as with cocaine, it is impracticable to maintain the threesyllable (p)t6'main.
===
**ptomaine.** The OED stigmatizes tomā'n as an illiterate pronunciation; but, as with *cocaine*, it is impracticable to maintain the three-syllable (p)tō'ma͞in.
>>>

